  "review_notes": {
    "reviewer": "jonf",
    "reviewed_at": "2026-07-27",
    "confidence": "low",
    "summary": "Budapest District V was by a wide margin the hardest split to ground-truth, and this pass should be read as a rough signal, not a measurement. The pedestrian infrastructure differs enough from the US cities the labeling rubric grew up on that 'is this a curb ramp?' was an open question on a large share of panos rather than an edge case — it arguably needs its own rubric, not the US one stretched to fit. One reviewer, one pass, no adjudication, and the reviewer rates their own consistency here as low: a second rater applying the same rubric could plausibly land on materially different recall. The relative signal (RampNet does markedly worse here than on US splits) looks real; the exact values should not be dropped into a table next to the US cities without this caveat traveling with them.",
    "caveats": [
      "Sweeping diagonal corner ramps. Budapest corners frequently carry a single broad ramped apron spanning much of the corner — far larger than a typical US curb ramp and serving two crossing directions at once. These were generally marked as TWO ramps, one per direction of travel. That is a rubric decision, not an observation, and it is the single largest source of uncertainty in this split. Interestingly RampNet frequently did the same thing unprompted. Where a second detection on one apron looked purely redundant it was marked 'duplicate' instead — so the same physical geometry did not always get scored the same way within this pass.",
      "That one call is worth ~4 points of precision. All-panos: 0.873 with duplicates scored as false positives (the default) vs 0.910 with --lenient-duplicates. This split has 7 duplicate marks where richmond and bend have 1 each and clovis and morgantown have none — the quantitative fingerprint of the ramp-apron ambiguity. 6 of the 7 fall in the 5 hand-picked 'top' panos, so the unbiased subset barely moves (0.885 vs 0.891); quote which duplicate scoring you used.",
      "Curb ramp vs. intended pedestrian path. Many District V surfaces are genuinely ambiguous between a curb ramp and a continuously graded pedestrian route or crossing that never presents a curb to ramp down at all. The US-derived rubric does not cleanly decide these, and the reviewer's line between them almost certainly drifted over the course of the session.",
      "Highest abstention rate of any split, which is the honest quantitative shadow of the two caveats above: 16 of 189 detections (8.5%) marked unsure, and 48 of 197 missed-ramp marks (24.4%) marked unsure. Both abstain from precision and recall rather than being forced into a guess.",
      "Imagery quality is NOT the explanation for the low numbers. 124 of 125 panos are Mapillary GoPro Max — the same camera as morgantown, which scores P 0.975 — mostly 5760x2880, median quality score 0.869, and 118 of 125 captured 2025-09 or later (the freshest imagery in the benchmark), across 92 sequences from 3 contributors. Whatever depresses precision and recall here is the city and the rubric, not sensor sharpness.",
      "Recall 0.503 (0.459 unbiased) sits far below every other split (0.713-0.765) and rests on 149 confident missed marks, roughly double any other split's (bend 79, richmond 73, morgantown 72, clovis 56). Part of that is a real domain gap — the model was trained on US curb ramps — and part is that the reviewer counted more things as ramps here. This pass alone cannot separate the two.",
      "What would actually settle this: a Budapest-specific rubric written from Hungarian street design rather than adapted from the US one, and a second independent rater on the same 125 panos to measure how much of the recall gap is the model and how much is the labeler. Of every split in the benchmark, this is the one most in need of a second opinion."
    ],
    "see": "benchmark/README.md - 'Budapest District V' section"
  },
